partialText:
&lt;p&gt;You get up for a coffee, answer the door or grab something from the printer. Five minutes later Teams has switched you to &lt;strong&gt;Away&lt;/strong&gt;, even though you're back at your desk a minute after that.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The obvious fix is one of the many "keep awake" tools: &lt;code&gt;caffeinate&lt;/code&gt; or Amphetamine on a Mac, PowerToys Awake on Windows. They don't help: Teams still goes yellow. So I dug into why, and ended up writing a small app, &lt;a href="https://github.com/christianmusumeci00/KeepMeOn" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;KeepMeOn&lt;/a&gt;, for macOS and Windows. This post is about what I learned: there are two different "idle" concepts in an operating system, and keep-awake tools only touch one of them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Two kinds of "idle"
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1. Power management idle.&lt;/strong&gt; This decides when the display dims, when the screen saver starts and when the computer goes to sleep. Apps can hold it off with a power assertion:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;macOS: &lt;code&gt;IOPMAssertionCreateWithName&lt;/code&gt; (what &lt;code&gt;caffeinate&lt;/code&gt; and most keep-awake apps use) or &lt;code&gt;IOPMAssertionDeclareUserActivity&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Windows: &lt;code&gt;SetThreadExecutionState(ES_SYSTEM_REQUIRED | ES_DISPLAY_REQUIRED)&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2. Input idle time.&lt;/strong&gt; This is a plain counter: how long since the last mouse, keyboard or trackpad event. Any app can read it:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;macOS: &lt;code&gt;CGEventSourceSecondsSinceLastEventType&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Windows: &lt;code&gt;GetLastInputInfo&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Teams decides you're Away after roughly five minutes in which that &lt;strong&gt;input&lt;/strong&gt; counter keeps growing. Power assertions don't reset it. They keep the screen on and the machine awake, but as far as the input counter is concerned, nobody has touched the computer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I measured it rather than trusting the docs. On a Windows 11 machine, calling &lt;code&gt;SetThreadExecutionState&lt;/code&gt; did nothing to the input idle time: it kept climbing. On macOS, &lt;code&gt;IOPMAssertionDeclareUserActivity&lt;/code&gt; postponed display sleep but left the counter Teams reads untouched.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the usual workarounds do
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Keep-awake apps&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;caffeinate&lt;/code&gt;, Amphetamine, PowerToys Awake): they hold a power assertion. Great for long downloads or presentations, but the input counter still grows, so Teams still goes Away.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mouse jigglers:&lt;/strong&gt; they move the cursor a pixel back and forth. That does reset the counter, but it moves your actual cursor, can wake hover menus and tooltips, and fights with you if you come back mid-jiggle.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pressing a real key&lt;/strong&gt; (F15, Shift‚Ä¶): resets the counter too, but some apps react to it, and holding a modifier at the wrong moment turns your next click into a Shift-click.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What I wanted was an input event that resets the counter while every app ignores it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  macOS: an "unchanged modifiers" event
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;macOS has an event type for modifier key changes, &lt;code&gt;flagsChanged&lt;/code&gt;. It fires when you press or release ‚åò, ‚å•, ‚áß, ‚åÉ or Caps Lock, and it carries the full set of modifiers that are now down.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you post a &lt;code&gt;flagsChanged&lt;/code&gt; event whose flags are &lt;em&gt;exactly the current flags&lt;/em&gt;, nothing changes for any app: no key went down, no key went up, no character was typed. But it's still an input event, so the idle counter resets.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight swift"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;postNeutralEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;Bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;CGEventSource&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;flagsState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;combinedSessionState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Skip it if the user is holding a modifier right now.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;held&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;CGEventFlags&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;maskShift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;maskControl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;maskAlternate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;maskCommand&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;maskSecondaryFn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;guard&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;CGPreflightPostEventAccess&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(),&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;intersection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;held&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;isEmpty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="k"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;CGEvent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;CGEventSource&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;stateID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;hidSystemState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

    &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;flagsChanged&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;flags&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;            &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// same modifiers as now: at most Caps Lock&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;setIntegerValueField&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;eventSourceUserData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nv"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mh"&gt;0x4B4D_4F4E&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// "KMON" tag&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;event&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;tap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;cghidEventTap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A few details that matter:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It needs the Accessibility permission.&lt;/strong&gt; Posting events is gated by macOS; &lt;code&gt;CGPreflightPostEventAccess()&lt;/code&gt; tells you whether you have it, and &lt;code&gt;CGRequestPostEventAccess()&lt;/code&gt; adds the app to the Accessibility list. macOS checks the permission when the app launches, so after granting it you have to relaunch.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It never runs while a modifier is held.&lt;/strong&gt; If you're in the middle of a ‚åò-drag, the app doesn't touch anything.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It's tagged.&lt;/strong&gt; The &lt;code&gt;eventSourceUserData&lt;/code&gt; field carries a marker, so anyone watching with an event tap can tell these events apart from real input.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I still declare user activity with &lt;code&gt;IOPMAssertionDeclareUserActivity&lt;/code&gt; as well: on its own it doesn't keep you green, but it does stop the display from sleeping, which you want too.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Windows: the key-up of a key that doesn't exist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Windows has a range of virtual key codes that aren't assigned to anything: &lt;code&gt;0x97&lt;/code&gt; to &lt;code&gt;0x9F&lt;/code&gt;. If you send a &lt;strong&gt;key-up&lt;/strong&gt; for one of them with &lt;code&gt;SendInput&lt;/code&gt;, no app reacts, because there's no such key and it was never pressed. For Windows it still counts as input, so &lt;code&gt;GetLastInputInfo&lt;/code&gt; resets.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight csharp"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;ushort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;UnassignedKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0x97&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kt"&gt;var&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;INPUT&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;INPUT_KEYBOARD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;InputUnion&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;ki&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;KEYBDINPUT&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;wVk&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;UnassignedKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;dwFlags&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;KEYEVENTF_KEYUP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="n"&gt;dwExtraInfo&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;IntPtr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;0x4B4D4F4E&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// "KMON" tag&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="nf"&gt;SendInput&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;input&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Marshal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;SizeOf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;INPUT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)));&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;On Windows, no permission is needed. On a Windows 11 test machine this brought the idle time from 144 s to 0.3 s without moving the cursor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I also tried a zero-pixel mouse move (&lt;code&gt;MOUSEEVENTF_MOVE&lt;/code&gt; with &lt;code&gt;dx = dy = 0&lt;/code&gt;). It works too, but I went with the unassigned key-up. One limitation: if an app running as administrator is in the foreground, Windows' UIPI protection can block injected input from a normal-privilege process.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To make sure this keeps working, the GitHub Actions workflow runs a small probe on a real Windows runner. It sends each candidate signal and checks that the idle counter resets and the cursor doesn't move.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Only when you're actually away
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Resetting the counter every minute would be easy and wrong: it would hide the fact that you're actually gone for an hour. So the app has a few rules:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It only acts after 2 minutes without real input.&lt;/strong&gt; While you work, it does nothing at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It checks every 60 seconds.&lt;/strong&gt; With these defaults, the idle counter peaks at about 3 minutes, comfortably under Teams' 5. (In a 7.5-minute run without input on a Mac, the highest value I saw was 181 s.)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;It pauses&lt;/strong&gt; when the screen is locked, when the computer or display is asleep, and optionally outside work hours and during a lunch break.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;If you're leaving for longer, lock the screen.&lt;/strong&gt; KeepMeOn stops, and Teams correctly shows you as Away.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last point is the whole idea: it's for the coffee break, not for pretending to be at your desk all afternoon. If your computer is managed by your company, check that this kind of tool is allowed. KeepMeOn stays visible on purpose: running process, login item and, on a Mac, an entry in the Accessibility list.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The app
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/christianmusumeci00/KeepMeOn" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;KeepMeOn&lt;/a&gt; is free and open source (MIT):&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mac:&lt;/strong&gt; native Swift and SwiftUI menu bar app, under 1 MB, macOS 13 or later, signed and notarized.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Windows:&lt;/strong&gt; C# WinForms tray app on .NET Framework 4.8, which ships with Windows 10 and 11. The installer is about 2 MB.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No dependencies, no account, no analytics. It only contacts GitHub to check for updates, and you can turn that off.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;English and Italian.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you've found a cleaner way to reset the input idle time, or a case where these signals don't work, I'd love to hear about it in the comments.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;I used AI coding tools while building the app and writing this post. The measurements and the code are from the project's repository.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A small edit can look like a new skill. Measure the failure you removed before you call the score learning.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the part worth stealing from a Hacker News thread about models that read and write neural network weights directly. Researchers at MIT CSAIL asked GPT-6 Astra to make Qwen3.5-0.8B better at math by editing weights. No gradients. No training loop. Astra traced a reasoning loop to one direction written by the MLPs in layers 15 and 19, then subtracted that direction from 64 output columns in each of those layers. The patch is 131,072 weights. On a model they describe as having 0.8 billion weights, that is 0.016%.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The scoreboard is easy to screenshot and easy to strip of context. MATH-500 went from 7.9% to 48.1%. GSM8K went from 13.8% to 67.1%. IFEval, which measures instruction following, went from 21.0% to 62.3%. Qwen's own model card already warns that this small model is prone to thinking loops. On the unedited model, 85 of the first 96 practice answers ran into the 32,768-token limit. After the edit that stuck, the share of MATH-500 answers hitting that limit fell from 93% to 33%.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The attempts that missed are the lesson. With 10 forward passes, MATH-500 only reached 12.3%, and 87.2% of answers still died at the cap. With 25 passes, 14.1% and 86.4%. The score did not climb while the model learned math. It sat still until the loop broke, and then it jumped. The loop is not special to math, which is why a non-math test moved too. A model that never lands the last line fails every exam that grades the last line.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The authors then ran the control that separates a blocker from a skill. Gemma 3 270M rarely loops. They report that only 8.5% of its answers reach its token limit, against 93% for that Qwen. Same style of direct weight edit. MATH-500 started at 6.8%, and no edited run moved it by more than about a point. The best figure in their table is 7.7%. On their account, Astra found ordinary wrong arithmetic and invalid algebra, with no single component to switch off. Fixing a loop lets the model finish answers it could already work out. Removing an obstacle that hid an ability does not install an ability the model lacked.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same shape shows up in systems that have no weights at all.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Suppose an API's success rate climbs after you delete one retry bug: on timeout, call yourself until the gateway hangs up. Callers start getting a response. The handler did not get wiser. It stopped spending the whole deadline on a polite panic. Requests that were wrong for a real reason are still wrong. Success rate cannot tell those two stories apart. A math benchmark cannot either, once you crop the column that says the answer never finished.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Or take a function that returns early on a weird input, so the suite is a pile of timeouts. You delete the early return. The tests go green. The changelog says the score jumped. What jumped was termination. The missing branch is still missing. It is only visible now. That is worth shipping, and it is still not a new algorithm.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Do not publish the headline number and leave the blocker in a footnote you drop from the slide.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;MATH-500 and "hit the token limit" are one measurement. Ten and twenty-five passes barely moved either one. Fifty passes moved both. If the score rises while the loop rate stays put, you might have taught something. If they move together, you unblocked a finish. Deleting &lt;code&gt;while (true)&lt;/code&gt; is not a math curriculum. It is a favor to whoever pays for the electricity.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Before you trust a delta tomorrow, write the suspected blocker in one sentence, in the language of the system you have.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"It repeats until the sampler stops." "It retries until the gateway kills the request." "The test times out because we return before the hard case." Log that rate beside the score, on the same examples, before and after. Then find a control where the blocker is already rare. If the control sits still, you removed an obstacle. Put the remaining errors on a real plan: data, training, or code that implements the case you do not have yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ship the unblocker. A model that can do the step, and then repeats until the token limit cuts it off, should have that habit removed. The patch was small because the habit was narrow. Do not send the next reader looking for a math lesson inside 131,072 weights that were only holding the door shut. The answers it could already work out were waiting behind that door.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Source: &lt;a href="https://rikiyat.github.io/blog/weight-space-programming/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://rikiyat.github.io/blog/weight-space-programming/&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Hacker News: &lt;a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50341174" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50341174&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post was generated automatically from public Hacker News data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TL;DR: A small Node.js team should choose a feature-toggle service by how cleanly it can connect a flag evaluation to a notification delivery failure, then attach that failure to a cost-bearing dimension. The useful comparison is not a price table. It is the quality of the evidence produced during a rollback: which cohort saw the change, which delivery path failed, how long it failed, and what it cost the media business. A hosted service, a self-hosted service, and a library can all be reasonable choices, but none of them supplies that accounting model for free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The page that wakes the on-call is usually the last page in the chain. It says that delivery failures have crossed the SLO budget, perhaps with a sharp rise in rejected push requests or email provider responses. The on-call sees a red panel, a deployment marker, and a question that the panel cannot answer: did the new notification experience cause the failure, or did an unrelated provider degrade?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For a media service, that distinction has an operational price. A failed breaking-news notification is different from a failed recommendation digest, even when both return a 5xx. The first step is to preserve the flag key, cohort, notification class, provider route, and attempt result as bounded dimensions. The next is to join those dimensions to delivery value outside the hot path. That is the decision rule I would use for a small team evaluating a toggle service in 2026.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What should the first alert tell the on-call?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It should identify a change in the user-visible outcome, not merely a change in application traffic. Start with an SLO for accepted notification attempts and another for successfully delivered attempts; define the error budget and the window before selecting a threshold. A feature flag can change the denominator, so a total-failure alert can hide a bad cohort behind a healthy control group.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The alert should answer four questions without a dashboard scavenger hunt:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which flag variation was evaluated?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Which notification class and delivery route were affected?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Did the failure happen before enqueue, during provider submission, or after an asynchronous result?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;What business-cost bucket receives the event?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That last field is deliberately not a dollar amount in the request path. Cost attribution belongs in a durable event or warehouse model, where finance and product can revise the value of a missed alert without redeploying the notification service. The request path needs a stable category such as &lt;code&gt;breaking_news&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;digest&lt;/code&gt;, or &lt;code&gt;account_security&lt;/code&gt;, plus an event identifier that permits reconciliation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the trap.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If the event is emitted only after a provider response, a queue outage disappears from the accounting model even though the user never reached the provider. If it is emitted before acceptance, a rejected request looks like a successful business event. The useful design records acceptance, transport outcome, and terminal delivery state separately, then gives each state an owner and a reconciliation rule.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the shape of a bounded event in Go. The same fields can be emitted by a Node.js service through a generic collector; the language is incidental, while the cardinality boundary is not.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;delivery&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;FailureEvent&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;FlagKey&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"flag_key"`&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Variant&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"variant"`&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Notification&lt;/span&gt;   &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"notification_class"`&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Route&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"delivery_route"`&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Stage&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"failure_stage"`&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;CostBucket&lt;/span&gt;     &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"cost_bucket"`&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;AttemptID&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;`json:"attempt_id"`&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Do not put recipient IDs, article IDs, or arbitrary error text into metric labels. Prometheus's instrumentation guidance calls out the operational risk of unbounded label cardinality. Put those identifiers in logs or traces, sample them where appropriate, and retain the bounded fields in the metric used for paging.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How do you work backward from the page to the earlier signal?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take one alert and draw the path in reverse. The page reports a delivery SLO breach. The SLO consumes a count of failed attempts. That count must distinguish a disabled route from a provider rejection, and both must retain the evaluated variant. The variant comes from a flag decision that should be recorded with the notification attempt, not inferred later from deployment timing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is where many small systems become misleading. A flag SDK may evaluate locally, remotely, or from a cached snapshot. Those modes have different failure behavior, so the notification service should expose evaluation freshness and fallback state as operational signals. A stale evaluation is not automatically an incident, but a rising stale-evaluation rate during a rollout is evidence that the experiment's cohorts are no longer what the operator thinks they are.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The instrumentation change is modest: create one correlation identifier at acceptance, carry it through enqueue and provider submission, and emit one terminal outcome. Retries must reference the same logical attempt while retaining an attempt count. Otherwise, a provider timeout followed by a successful retry appears as two unrelated business events, and the cost model charges the wrong thing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I would set a review threshold before rollout, not during the first page. For example, a team might pause a rollout when the treatment group's delivery error rate exceeds control by a pre-agreed relative margin for two consecutive evaluation windows. The exact margin depends on traffic and the SLO; the important part is that the rule names the comparison and the action. A single noisy provider response should not roll back a feature, but a sustained cohort-specific breach should not wait for a full daily report.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Which service boundary actually matters for a small team?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The practical comparison is a set of responsibilities, not a leaderboard. A hosted toggle platform usually reduces the amount of control-plane operation the team owns, while introducing dependency on its availability, SDK behavior, and commercial terms. An open-source control plane can make data placement and customization easier to reason about, but the team inherits upgrades, backups, access control, and incident response. A library or a small internal evaluator offers a narrow dependency surface and leaves targeting, audit history, and distribution correctness to the team. The limitation is plain: each boundary moves work; none removes it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Boundary&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Strength&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Cost attribution question&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Failure to test&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hosted control plane&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Less infrastructure to operate&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Can exported evaluations be joined to delivery outcomes with a stable flag and variant identity?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Control-plane or SDK unavailability during a rollout&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Self-hosted control plane&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;More control over data location and lifecycle&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Who owns retention, reconciliation, and the audit trail?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Stale replicas and an untested restore&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;In-process evaluator&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Small runtime surface and predictable local reads&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Where do targeting rules, approvals, and historical decisions live?&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A bad snapshot or unsafe default during deploy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three real products illustrate the boundary without settling the choice. LaunchDarkly is a hosted feature-management service with SDK-based evaluation and operational controls. Unleash publishes an open-source feature management project and also documents hosted and self-hosted deployment paths. Flagsmith documents both a hosted product and an open-source option. Those are materially different operating models, but the public product boundary still does not answer how a media team should value a missed notification or reconcile retries. That work remains an application and data-model responsibility.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hosted option is a poor fit when the team cannot accept an external control-plane dependency during an incident. Self-hosting is a poor fit when nobody owns restores, upgrades, and access reviews. An in-process evaluator is a poor fit when approval history and complex targeting are requirements rather than future work. Those are limitations of the operating choices, not reasons to declare one product the winner.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For pricing, record the variables that can change the decision: seats, environments, evaluated contexts, request volume, retention, support level, and whether the team must operate a control plane. Do not turn a current price page into an architectural conclusion. The relevant question is how a recurring charge compares with the on-call hours and failure modes the selected boundary creates, using the team's own traffic and SLO obligations.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What does a rollback-safe cost model look like?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keep the rollout decision and the accounting decision separate. The rollout path needs a fast, deterministic default and a clear kill switch. The accounting path needs enough durable context to explain the result later. If the flag service is unreachable, the notification path should follow a documented safe default for that flag; it should not invent a new cohort or silently classify the event as control.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use a 15-minute review window as a concrete starting point, then adjust it to traffic and error-budget burn. The window is a decision aid, not a universal threshold.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The event ledger can then aggregate by bounded dimensions:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight go"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;package&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ledger&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;CostKey&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Notification&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Variant&lt;/span&gt;      &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Route&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Stage&lt;/span&gt;        &lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Aggregate&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;struct&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Attempts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;uint64&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Failures&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;uint64&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;Retries&lt;/span&gt;  &lt;span class="kt"&gt;uint64&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;func&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Add&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Aggregate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;failed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retried&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Aggregate&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Attempts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;failed&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Failures&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;retried&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;Retries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;This intentionally stops short of pretending that every failure has one universally correct price. A missed security alert, a missed headline, and a delayed digest can have different business consequences. The model should expose those categories and let the owning teams attach reviewed values. It should also report uncertainty: a provider response may establish submission failure without proving that the recipient never saw the message.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Client experience belongs in the same review. If the flag changes a page that triggers notification enrollment, monitor the enrollment path and relevant web performance signals separately. Core Web Vitals guidance uses LCP, CLS, and INP with percentile-based thresholds, including p75, which is a useful reminder that averages can conceal a bad tail. Do not claim that a good page metric proves delivery health; use it as adjacent evidence when the rollout changes both surfaces.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How should the team test the decision before buying or building?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Run a narrow failure exercise against the whole trace: evaluate a flag, accept a notification, fail the provider call, retry it, emit the terminal event, and inspect the alert and ledger. Then repeat with a stale evaluation cache and with the control plane unavailable. The pass condition is not that every component stays healthy. It is that the team can identify the affected cohort, apply the documented default, and explain the resulting cost bucket.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The capacity question is easy to postpone and expensive to discover during an incident. Estimate peak evaluation rate, event volume from retries, retention, and the number of environments before selecting a service boundary. A small team can tolerate a narrow control plane and still fail operationally if its observability pipeline cannot absorb a retry storm. Conversely, a sophisticated targeting service is excess machinery if the team cannot review its flag inventory and remove expired rules.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The final selection should therefore be a written decision record with three gates: the SLO and rollback rule, the evidence needed for cost attribution, and the operational work the team agrees to own. Prices can be attached to that record and revisited. The evidence contract should not be negotiable after the first incident.&lt;/p&gt;

