&lt;p&gt;Learning software engineering is an absolute ride. Some days you feel like your coding IQ is increasing exponentially; other days, you wish you were just born with all the skills already downloaded into your brain!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Lately, I’ve been making the transition from core Java OOP concepts into Spring Boot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I get the concepts… but at the same time, I don’t quite get them yet. Funny, right?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  💻 Where I’m At Right Now
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I've been following along with structured literature, working through hands-on code examples line by line. Right now, I'm exploring Spring Context configurations, * lazy * initialization (*&lt;a class="mentioned-user" href="https://dev.to/lazy"&gt;@lazy&lt;/a&gt;), *component stereotypes, and fetching beans dynamically via ApplicationContext.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fq4euu9h5cw53bvlwgwt0.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fq4euu9h5cw53bvlwgwt0.png" alt=" " width="800" height="773"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  🧠 The "Real-World Project" Gap
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Even though the book examples run smoothly, I find myself pausing and asking:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;"How does this actually map to a real, production-ready system?"&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Because textbook exercises are isolated, it can be tricky to discern why a specific design choice is made until you’re building something substantial from scratch. I suspect the missing bridge is simply experience—I haven't built a massive end-to-end production application in Spring yet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Despite the hurdles, the process has been genuinely fun. Watching how framework abstractions work after months of pure Java feels like discovering magic spells.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  ❓ To the Spring Devs out there: What was your turning point?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For anyone who has crossed this bridge before:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When did the "Spring Way" of thinking finally click for you? Was it during your first full project, or did a specific concept make it all fall into place?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What small starter project would you recommend for bridging the gap between textbook examples and real-world backend architecture?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you're currently in the middle of learning Spring too, let me know how your journey is going in the comments below!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I'm building AxiomStory, a story studio for novelists, screenwriters and worldbuilders. You write or import your book, and it builds a wiki of your characters and places. From there it can draw them, give them voices and turn scenes into short videos where they speak their lines.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The hardest part so far sounds boring. The same person has to look like the same person every time. Here's what broke and what I changed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The first bug was a missing picture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In October a test video came back with the hero wearing different clothes in the second shot. I assumed the image model was drifting. Then I looked at what we were actually sending.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Shot images only got the character's wiki picture if the writer had ticked a box saying it matched. Almost nobody ticked it, so most shots were drawn from a text description alone. Worse, the screen showed the picture next to the shot, so it looked like it was being used.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now every shot attaches the character's reference sheet by default, and the writer can take one off with a click.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Names are messier than you'd think
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The next miss was a character called Cael. His wiki page was titled with his full name, and the shot text just said "Cael". No match, no picture.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Books are full of this. People pick up nicknames and titles, and the narrator switches between them halfway through a chapter. Matching now checks every name and alias on the wiki page, so a shot that says "Cael" finds the right sheet.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  One sheet, one person
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Each main character gets one reference sheet: several views of the same person in a single image. It's cheaper than separate pictures, and the views stay consistent with each other.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The catch is that a grid of figures looks like a group of people to a model. When a shot had two characters, I was sending what looked like a crowd. The fix was one plain line before the story text: "Attached character sheets, one per person: A; B." That was enough.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fk8x4bmg3ryx1h5i9zuf4.jpg" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fk8x4bmg3ryx1h5i9zuf4.jpg" alt="Character sheet views: Phill, then Mara" width="800" height="258"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Views from Phill's sheet, then Mara. One sheet per person, sent with every shot they appear in.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Words fight pictures
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My early prompts pasted the character's whole wiki bio into every shot. It felt thorough. In practice the text and the picture would disagree on small things like hair length or a coat, and the model would split the difference.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now shot prompts carry the action and the lines, and the sheet carries the look. I also stopped asking for props the sheet doesn't show. In one take a sword slowly turned into a book pouch, because the sheet had no sword to copy.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  I tried chaining shots and took it out
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The obvious trick is to give each shot the previous shot's still. I built it and ran an A/B, and faces did hold a little better. But every shot had to wait for the one before it, and one bad frame could carry forward into the rest of the scene.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I went the other way. Every shot is drawn at once, in parallel, and each one is anchored to the same wiki sheets. If one shot comes out wrong, you redo that one shot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8w9nyumhw17bljkfjm5x.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F8w9nyumhw17bljkfjm5x.png" alt="Seven video takes from one scene, all marked In cut" width="800" height="479"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;A 31 second scene in seven shots. Each one was drawn on its own from the same sheets, and they still match.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The house style is words only
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I wanted every picture in the app to share one look: flat editorial illustration with simple backgrounds. The easy way would be to send a style reference image with every request. I didn't, because I was worried it would bleed into the characters themselves.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The style lives in one file as a short description, and the same text feeds wiki pictures and video frames. A test checks that the web app and the API use identical wording, so they can't quietly drift apart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Frpnwrvlvqj0cziaddjbw.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Frpnwrvlvqj0cziaddjbw.png" alt="Two wordings of the house style on the same two scenes" width="800" height="533"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Two wordings of the house style on the same two scenes. I kept the left one: simpler faces, flatter colour, plainer backgrounds.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I'd tell someone building this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Most of my consistency bugs came down to plumbing: a reference that never got sent, or a name that didn't match. Check what you're actually sending before you swap models.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you write fiction and want to see your cast drawn the same way twice, you can try it at &lt;a href="https://axiomstory.com" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;axiomstory.com&lt;/a&gt;. Start free, no card.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;You found an N+1 in your GraphQL API: a list of 25 posts, each &lt;code&gt;Post.author&lt;/code&gt; resolver running its own &lt;code&gt;SELECT&lt;/code&gt;. You added a DataLoader, the query count dropped from 26 to 2, and you moved on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the batch function most people write first:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;userById&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DataLoader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;User&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;User&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ANY($1::uuid[])&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It passes every test you throw at it. It is also wrong in two ways, and one of them is silent.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Bug 1: Postgres doesn't return rows in the order you asked
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;DataLoader has one contract: the batch function returns an array &lt;strong&gt;in the same order and of the same length&lt;/strong&gt; as &lt;code&gt;ids&lt;/code&gt;. Result 0 goes to whoever asked for &lt;code&gt;ids[0]&lt;/code&gt;, result 1 to &lt;code&gt;ids[1]&lt;/code&gt;, and so on. DataLoader never looks inside the objects to check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;WHERE id = ANY($1)&lt;/code&gt; makes no promise about order. Postgres returns rows in whatever order its plan produces them: heap order for a sequential scan, index order for an index scan. Neither is the order of your array.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So if DataLoader asks for &lt;code&gt;[u3, u1, u2]&lt;/code&gt; and Postgres returns &lt;code&gt;[u1, u2, u3]&lt;/code&gt;, the post that asked for &lt;code&gt;u3&lt;/code&gt; gets &lt;code&gt;u1&lt;/code&gt;, and every other author shifts too. No error, no warning, valid-looking data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Why your tests didn't catch it: in a test database with three freshly inserted users, a small sequential scan usually returns them in insertion order, and your test probably requests them in that same order. In production, with updates moving rows around and the planner picking an index scan, the order drifts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Bug 2: one missing row breaks the whole batch
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Say one post references a deleted user. Now Postgres returns 24 rows for 25 ids. DataLoader checks the length, sees a mismatch, and &lt;strong&gt;rejects every key in the batch&lt;/strong&gt;. One dangling foreign key turns into 25 errors and a list that won't render.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The fix: map rows back to keys
&lt;/h2&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;byKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;K&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;keys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;K&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[],&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;keyOf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;K&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;T&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)[]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;keyOf&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]));&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;keys&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;k&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;userById&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DataLoader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;User&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;User&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ANY($1::uuid[])&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;byKey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Same order as &lt;code&gt;ids&lt;/code&gt;, same length, &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt; for a missing row. The field resolver decides what &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt; means (a nullable &lt;code&gt;author&lt;/code&gt; field, or a &lt;code&gt;NOT_FOUND&lt;/code&gt; error) instead of the whole batch failing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For one-to-many fields (&lt;code&gt;Order.items&lt;/code&gt;), group instead of map, and return &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt; for keys with no rows:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;itemsByOrderId&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;DataLoader&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;OrderItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;orderIds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;query&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;OrderItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;SELECT * FROM order_items WHERE order_id = ANY($1::uuid[]) ORDER BY created_at&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;orderIds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;groups&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;OrderItem&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;groups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;groups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;order_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;list&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;orderIds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;groups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h2&gt;


  Two more ways DataLoader leaks data
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A loader at module level.&lt;/strong&gt; DataLoader caches every key it loads, forever, for the life of the loader. Create it once at import time and user A's request warms a cache that user B's request reads from: stale rows at best, rows B shouldn't see at worst. Create loaders in the per-request context factory:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;context&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;loaders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createLoaders&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Authorization inside the batch function.&lt;/strong&gt; A batch mixes keys from every resolver in the request, and the cache is keyed by id only. If the batch function filters by "can the viewer see this?", the answer gets cached under the id and reused. Load the row, then check access in the resolver.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Test the order, not just the count
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The usual DataLoader test counts queries. Add one that would have caught Bug 1: request keys in an order the database won't naturally return.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;it&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;returns each post with its own author&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createUsers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Posts reference users in reverse insertion order&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;users&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;reverse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createPost&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;authorId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;u&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;execute&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`{ posts(first: 5) { authorId author { id } } }`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;res&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;posts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nf"&gt;expect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;author&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;toBe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;post&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;authorId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Run it against a real Postgres. A mocked &lt;code&gt;db.query&lt;/code&gt; returns whatever order you wrote in the mock, which is exactly the assumption that's wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Checklist
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Batch functions map rows back to keys; they never &lt;code&gt;return rows&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Missing rows become &lt;code&gt;null&lt;/code&gt; (or &lt;code&gt;[]&lt;/code&gt;), not a shorter array.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Loaders are created per request, never at module level.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Access checks run in resolvers, after loading.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;One test asserts each item gets its own related row, in non-insertion order.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Making Claude Code write it this way
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ask Claude Code to "fix the N+1 with DataLoader" and you'll often get the &lt;code&gt;return rows&lt;/code&gt; version, because it looks finished. I wrote this pattern up as a Claude Code skill that finds N+1s, builds per-request loaders with the key mapping above, and adds a query-count test.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;That skill is free, along with a safe Postgres migrations skill: &lt;a href="https://github.com/adnan909/free-sample" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;github.com/adnan909/free-sample&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The full pack adds 7 more skills (cursor pagination, typed errors, resolver auth, integration tests and more) and a starter backend where all of it runs in CI: &lt;a href="https://adnology0.gumroad.com/l/backend-skills-pack" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Backend Skills Pack on Gumroad&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Have you hit the wrong-order bug in production? I'd like to hear how long it took to spot.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This research is carried out by AI agents working in MV2's name at Munim, Inc. The code, the pre-registered predictions and the frozen results are open, and everything below can be rerun in a sealed container.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our last audit found that on 46 MMLU-Pro questions whose answer key MMLU-Redux 2.0's experts had marked wrong, all 45 published models we read pick the experts' answer more often than the key. That raised an obvious question. MMLU-Redux's labels reach only about 40% of MMLU-Pro's 6,810 MMLU-derived questions (2,829) and none of its other 5,222. Could agreement among the strongest models find wrong keys everywhere else?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We wrote down a simple rule and six predictions before running anything, then tested the rule where experts had already looked. Four predictions held and two failed. The failures are the most useful part, and a second pre-registered audit then tested the most likely explanation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The rule
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;TIGER-AI-Lab, which maintains MMLU-Pro, publishes every model's answer to every question. We took the 10 most accurate of the 45 usable archives (Gemini 3.1 Pro at 91.1% down to Gemini 1.5 Pro at 70.3%). A question is &lt;strong&gt;flagged&lt;/strong&gt; when at least 6 of those 10 pick the same option other than the key, and more of them pick it than pick the key. None of these models ever saw MMLU-Redux's labels, so the experts are an independent check.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What we found
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Group (questions)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Flagged&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Experts say the key is wrong (55)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;strong&gt;36 (65.5%)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Experts found another error (117)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;29 (24.8%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Experts say the question is fine (2,553)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;294 (11.5%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;From MMLU, never reviewed by the experts (3,928)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;501 (12.75%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;From the STEM website source (4,083)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;271 (6.64%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;All of MMLU-Pro (12,032)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1,210 (10.1%)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What held.&lt;/strong&gt; The flag fires on questions the experts call miskeyed 5.7 times as often as on questions they cleared (we predicted at least 5). It catches 34 of the 46 inherited miskeys from our last audit, and on 31 of those the models' choice is exactly the experts' answer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What failed.&lt;/strong&gt; We predicted that at least 20% of flagged, expert-reviewed questions would carry some expert error label. It was 18.1% (65 of 359). Only 10% (36) are wrong keys. So at this threshold, about four in five flags land on questions the experts cleared. They may be questions where strong models share a mistake, questions with a second defensible answer, or errors the experts missed. This data alone cannot tell which.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The other failure.&lt;/strong&gt; MMLU-Pro's own paper names the STEM website source as a place where wrong keys came from, so we predicted those questions would be flagged more. They are flagged half as often as MMLU-derived ones (6.64% vs 12.83%), at every threshold we looked at. Either their keys are cleaner, or wrong answers to numeric questions spread across options instead of agreeing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Stricter is better, but rarer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;After the run we also looked at stricter rules (not pre-registered). With all 10 models agreeing, 201 questions are flagged, 21 of the 55 expert miskeys are caught, and 36.4% of flagged expert-reviewed questions carry an error label. Requiring more agreement buys precision at the cost of recall.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Follow-up: is it the options MMLU-Pro added?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The experts reviewed MMLU's four options. MMLU-Pro kept those four and added up to six more per question, and its paper reports that its own review removed 1,953 added options from the MMLU part that were also correct. If some remain, a strong model would pick one, and the experts' "OK" would not cover it. So we pre-registered a second audit (78): map each MMLU-derived question back to MMLU, label every option as one of MMLU's original four or one MMLU-Pro added, and look at which kind the panel agrees on. 5,818 questions keep all four MMLU options and were classified.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Control, held.&lt;/strong&gt; Where the experts say the key is wrong, the panel's choice is one of MMLU's own options in &lt;strong&gt;36 of 36&lt;/strong&gt; flagged questions, as it must be if the instrument works.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Headline, held.&lt;/strong&gt; Where the experts cleared the key, the panel agrees on an option MMLU-Pro added in &lt;strong&gt;185 of 290&lt;/strong&gt; flagged questions (63.8%). That is more often than the panel's scattered wrong answers land on added options elsewhere (56.7%).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;But only modestly.&lt;/strong&gt; Added options are 64.1% of all non-key options on those questions, so the consensus lands on them at about the rate their number alone would give. And &lt;strong&gt;105 of the 290&lt;/strong&gt; flags point at one of MMLU's own distractors, which the experts saw and still cleared. Added options explain part of the gap, not all of it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Failed.&lt;/strong&gt; We expected law to show the effect most. It splits evenly (69 of 139 flagged law questions on an added option, against 61.9% elsewhere).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Four questions to re-check
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On four of the 46 inherited miskeys, &lt;strong&gt;all 10 top models pick MMLU-Pro's key over the experts' answer&lt;/strong&gt;: ids 6639 (virology), 8972 (abstract algebra), 9677 (astronomy) and 9947 (conceptual physics). Three of the four depend on how we read MMLU-Redux's corrections written as bare digits. These are the first expert labels a human should check again.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Limits
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model agreement is evidence, not proof.&lt;/strong&gt; Strong models can share a misconception.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The 10 panel slots are not 10 independent models.&lt;/strong&gt; Three are Claude 3.5 Sonnet runs, two are GPT-4o versions and two are runs labelled &lt;code&gt;arx&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The experts reviewed MMLU's four options, not MMLU-Pro's ten.&lt;/strong&gt; The follow-up shows this matters for about two in three flags on cleared questions, but it does not show that those added options are correct. That needs a human.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;All 1,210 flagged ids are in &lt;code&gt;candidates.json&lt;/code&gt; in the first audit's folder, and the 455 MMLU-derived questions whose consensus is an added option are in &lt;code&gt;added_candidates.json&lt;/code&gt; in the second. They are lists to review, not lists of errors. They hold ids, letters and counts only, no question or model text.&lt;/p&gt;

