&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;First published on &lt;a href="https://yappatyih.com/writing/google-63-billion-search-fewer-clicks/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;yappatyih.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  TL;DR
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Alphabet reported on 28 October 2026 that Google Search and other earned $63.35 billion in the third quarter, up 12% from a year earlier. That is about $690 million a day. Fewer clicks for your website can sit next to that number because the money comes from advertisers, and the clicks you are missing are the free ones. When Google's AI Overview answers a question on the results page, people click less. Being ranked is still needed, but on my own site, rank and being mentioned by AI did not move together. Start with three things: answer real customer questions on your website, keep your Google Business Profile updated, and ask happy customers for reviews.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Key takeaways
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Google's Search revenue is up 12%, and Google says query growth is the highest in five years. Its results do not mention clicks to websites at all.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Two independent studies, one of them a randomised experiment, found people click ordinary results far less when an AI summary appears.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;On my site, one search had me ranked second without being cited, and another had me cited without being in the top eight.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The same search can show an AI Overview one day and none the next, so one check tells you little.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You need no special file or markup. Google says so itself. The basics decide it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

